# TLS termination in front of cctraced (ADR-9). Used by
# deploy/docker-compose.tls.yml; cctraced itself keeps speaking plain HTTP and
# plain gRPC on the compose network.
#
# CADDY_TLS_MODE is `internal` by default: Caddy issues from its own CA, which
# needs no public DNS and works for a bare IP address. Clients then have to
# trust that CA. Point the cctrace profile at it once
# (`cctrace config set server.ca_cert_file <path>`) and cctrace writes it to
# its own HTTP client, to Claude Code as NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS with the exporter
# switched to http/protobuf on 5318, and to Codex's metrics exporter
# `tls.ca-certificate`. Measured on Claude Code 2.1.291, its grpc exporter
# trusted the CA through neither OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_CERTIFICATE nor
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS; http/protobuf with NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS did. Codex 0.160.0
# given no CA sent nothing over https and logged no error. Whether a CA
# installed only in the OS keychain is enough for either was not measured, so
# set the file even then. A running sync daemon keeps the CA it started with,
# so restart it after a change; Codex's block is rewritten when sync next starts.
#
# Export the CA with:
#   docker compose exec caddy cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
#
# For a publicly trusted certificate, set CADDY_TLS_MODE to an ACME account
# email address (e.g. ops@example.com) when the name resolves publicly and port
# 80 is reachable from the internet, and Caddy obtains one instead; cctrace and
# Claude Code then need no CA file, and Codex against a public certificate
# without one was not measured. The literal `acme` is not accepted: Caddy's tls
# directive takes `internal`, `force_automate` or an email, and refuses to start
# on anything else.
#
# default_sni is what makes a bare IP address work. A client connecting to an IP
# literal sends no SNI (RFC 6066 forbids IPs there), and without a default
# Caddy has no certificate to pick: the handshake ends in "tlsv1 alert internal
# error" even with the right CA. Measured against the dev stack on 2026-10-06:
# curl --cacert to https://<ip>:8443 failed that way, and the same request with
# the SNI forced succeeded; with default_sni, the cctrace client and Codex
# reached cctraced through Caddy without forcing anything. default_sni only
# names the certificate for a ClientHello that carries no SNI; a client that
# sends SNI, as clients of a hostname site address normally do, is matched by
# that name as before. Only the IP case above was measured.
{
	admin off
	default_sni {$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS}
}

# Dashboard and REST API.
{$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS}:8443 {
	tls {$CADDY_TLS_MODE}
	log
	reverse_proxy cctraced:8080
}

# OTLP over gRPC. h2c because the upstream speaks cleartext HTTP/2 -- without it
# Caddy would offer HTTP/1.1 to a listener that only understands h2, and the
# client sees a connection that opens and then stalls.
{$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS}:5317 {
	tls {$CADDY_TLS_MODE}
	log
	reverse_proxy h2c://cctraced:4317
}

# OTLP over HTTP.
{$CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS}:5318 {
	tls {$CADDY_TLS_MODE}
	log
	reverse_proxy cctraced:4318
}
